comments.body:
Unfortunately, whether you use local storage or cookies doesn't change the "cookie consent" problem, at least not from a privacy law perspective. The underlying directive covers all forms of storage. So this isn't really about disabling cookies so much as allowing users to opt-out of tracking at run time.

Your idea of using localStorage could provide a workaround, but the existing internal template would need to be amended to allow for that since it current (as of the v0.80.0 snap) generates Javascript code that reads:
```
var dnt = (navigator.doNotTrack || window.doNotTrack || navigator.msDoNotTrack);
var doNotTrack = (dnt == "1" || dnt == "yes");
if (!doNotTrack) {
...
```
which I can't manipulate from outside the template.

I just realized that there is no need for template changes. `doNotTrack` is a global variable that you can modify yourself.
```html
<script>
if (localStorage.getItem("doNotTrack") === null) {
    localStorage.setItem("doNotTrack", "1") // default value before any choice has been made
}
var doNotTrack = localStorage.getItem("doNotTrack");
</script>
{{ template "_internal/google_analytics.html" . }}
```

Update the localstorage item after consent, reload the page and you should be good to go. This works because `var doNotTrack` is the exact same variable as `window.doNotTrack`.

I've only tested it a little bit but it looks like it should work in Chrome, Safari, and Firefox.
